                        <!-- Core Principles of Christian Leadership -->
                        <div class="glass bg-cardDark p-6 sm:p-7 rounded-3xl border border-white/10 space-y-3 flex-1">
                            <div class="flex items-center gap-2.5 text-blue-400">
                                <span class="text-xl">✝️</span>
                                <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-white">Core Principles of Christian Leadership</h3>
                            </div>
                            <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
                                At the heart of the Upper Room ministry is the belief that Christian leadership is fundamentally defined as Christ-like influence. While the corporate world often views leadership through the lens of power, self-promotion, and upward mobility, the kingdom of God views it through the lens of downward mobility and service. Christ Himself modeled this, showing that true authority comes from laying down one's life for others.
                            </p>
                        </div>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <!-- Section: Shaping Vision & Intimacy as the Foundation -->
                <div class="glass bg-cardDark p-6 sm:p-8 rounded-3xl border border-white/10 space-y-6">
                    <div class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-6">
                        <div class="space-y-3">
                            <div class="flex items-center gap-2.5">
                                <span class="text-xl">🔭</span>
                                <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-white">Shaping Vision</h3>
                            </div>
                            <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
                                A leader without a vision is merely managing the status quo. Vision is the clear, challenging picture of the future of our ministry as it can and must be. According to <em>Leadership Essentials</em>, a compelling vision must be rooted in God's character and purposes, rather than our own ambition. It requires us to look beyond the immediate obstacles and articulate a hope-filled future that inspires the entire young adult community to action.
                            </p>
                        </div>

                        <div class="space-y-3">
                            <div class="flex items-center gap-2.5">
                                <span class="text-xl">🕊️</span>
                                <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-white">Intimacy as the Foundation</h3>
                            </div>
                            <p class="text-xs sm:text-sm text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
                                A genuine passion for Christ—who our teachings describe as the treasure of all knowledge and wisdom—is the engine of endurance. Without this foundational intimacy, leadership becomes mechanical, exhausting, and prone to burnout. We cannot lead others to a place we have not been ourselves; our public influence will only ever be as strong as our private devotion.
                            </p>
